BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Center for African Studies] Graduate Students Writing Session
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261125T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261125T113000
DTSTAMP:20261124T225803Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54322876786906-54322876815583@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Looking for a supportive intellectual community of humanities 
 scholars in African studies? \n\nJoin the Interdisciplinary Humanities Afr
 icanist Writing Group (IHAWG)! We meet every Wednesday from 9-11:30 a.m. i
 n the Center for African Studies (CAS)! Through co-writing sessions\, writ
 ing workshops\, and an exciting speaker series\, we hope to create a posit
 ive\, sustaining space for one another to grow as Africanist scholars. \n
 \nTo sign up for the mailing list or ask questions\, please reach out to t
 he f\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-11-24 14:58 PST
LOCATION:Encina Commons
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Office for Religious & Spiritual Life] Walk the Labyrinth at MemC
 hu
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261125T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261125T160000
DTSTAMP:20261124T225803Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54356689351868-54356689355968@local-events
DESCRIPTION:The labyrinth is an ancient pattern found in many cultures aro
 und the world. Labyrinth designs were found on poetry\, tables\, and tiles
  that date back as far as 5000 years.\n\nThe looping\, spiraling walking p
 ath rolled out onto the Stanford Memorial Church chancel every week is bas
 ed on the 12th-century stone labyrinth installed in the floor of the Cathe
 dral of Notre Dame in Chartres\, France. The rhythm and simplicity of foll
 owing a single path can quiet the mind\, restore balance\, facilitate medi
 ta\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-11-24 14:58 PST
LOCATION:Memorial Church
END:VEVENT
